<section class="tool-guide" aria-labelledby="word-to-wordpress-guide">
<h2 id="word-to-wordpress-guide">How to move a Word draft into WordPress</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Choose your document.</strong> Use the button above or the file control in Open your Word document. Select a plain .docx up to 8 MiB. The file is read locally; nothing is uploaded. Cancelling the picker keeps any existing writing and pending preview.</li><li><strong>Inspect the conversion.</strong> Check the reading preview, exact article text, page details, and every notice. The main article and supported referenced notes are converted. If an unmarked table starts with column names, explicitly choose the first-row header option and preview the selected file again.</li><li><strong>Import after review.</strong> If you need the embedded pictures, <a href="#keep-word-images">download the image package</a> before this step. Acknowledge the conversion, then choose <strong>Import and continue to export</strong> to open the WordPress handoff, or <strong>Import and edit</strong> to change the article first. Replacing existing writing asks for confirmation and checkpoints the previous valid workspace. Source notes are kept; article fields and review questions are replaced and export approval is cleared.</li><li><strong>Prepare the post.</strong> To combine another draft, choose Insert more writing below the article, preview the additional import, and choose its insertion location. Its body can join the article while existing page details and private notes stay in place. Use Back to editing whenever you need to revise the article. Compare facts with your source, inspect the WordPress handoff findings, and check the separate export review box. Copy or download WordPress blocks; the title stays separate. A complete WordPress kit is also available.</li><li><strong>Finish in WordPress.</strong> Create a new, empty draft post and enter the title in its title field. In Visual editor, click the empty paragraph below the title and paste with Ctrl+V or Command+V. Inspect the editable blocks, replace image placeholders, and add post settings. Save as a draft, close and reopen the post, and confirm the blocks remain editable without unexpected-content warnings. Preview on desktop and mobile before publishing yourself.</li></ol>
<p>If pasted source text appears, undo that paste or use another empty draft. Open the post Options menu, choose Code editor, and paste the complete source into the empty post body. Return to Visual editor and inspect again. Themes, plugins, and editor versions can affect the result. This is a manual handoff with a limited article format, not a complete Word layout conversion. <a href="/help/publishing-handoff/#wordpress-code-editor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Detailed fallback steps (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2 id="word-import-limits">What stays and what changes?</h2>
<div class="article-table" role="region" aria-label="Word import support, scroll horizontally if needed" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">In your Word document</th><th scope="col">In the article and WordPress handoff</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Paragraphs and common heading styles</td><td>Editable text and headings. An opening title heading moves to the separate title field. WordPress body H1 headings become H2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bold, italic, ordinary strikethrough, and safe web links</td><td>Single-line bold, italic, combined emphasis, ordinary single strikethrough, and absolute HTTP(S) hyperlinks with formatted labels. Unsupported styling may be simplified with notices; link availability is not checked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinary bullets and decimal lists</td><td>Editable lists with supported starts, continuation, and nesting. Up to six levels and 200 items per continuous group. Custom or lettered numbering needs simplification first.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Simple rectangular tables</td><td>Editable tables with a column-header row. Use Add or edit table below the article to change cells, rows, columns, and alignment without pipe-table syntax. Unmarked headers require an explicit import choice. Up to twelve columns and 100 data rows. Merged or nested tables are rejected.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Images and embedded objects</td><td>Numbered text placeholders. Before importing, optionally recover supported embedded PNG and JPEG files with their Word alternative text and placement notes. Upload the files and replace placeholders in WordPress. Linked images, other formats, and embedded objects need manual handling. For an image already online, Add or edit image can prepare its HTTPS URL; select linked images for WordPress separately.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headers, footers, author metadata, fonts, colors, and page layout</td><td>Excluded or simplified. Keep the original Word document and compare it with the preview.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Editorial comments</td><td>Excluded from article text. Use Recover Word comment text before importing to inspect and download private feedback, or explicitly keep eligible comments as private review notes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Footnotes and endnotes</td><td>Supported referenced notes become labeled article sections with visible markers. They are included in article exports. Review their numbering and text; WordPress receives ordinary blocks rather than native footnote objects.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tracked changes</td><td>Blocked by default. For ordinary main-article text insertions and deletions, explicitly preview proposed or previous text after choosing the file, then review before importing. Moves, formatting or structure changes, nested revisions, and revisions in notes still need review in Word. <a href="#word-text-revisions">Text revision limits</a>.</td></tr><tr><td>Fields, equations, and complex structures</td><td>Rejected before replacing writing. Resolve automatic tables of contents and unsupported structures in Word, or import a smaller formatted selection.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The converted article is limited to 60,000 characters. Old .doc files, macro-enabled or encrypted documents, damaged archives, and unusually packaged files are not supported. If compressed Word import is unavailable in your browser, copy a formatted selection instead. A rejected file can be discarded with Clear selected file or import preview.</p>
<!--WORD_NOTES_HELP-->
<h2 id="keep-word-images">Keep embedded Word images</h2>
<ol><li>While reviewing the selected Word file, choose <strong>Prepare Word image files</strong>. This separate local step reads supported image files; ordinary conversion reads document XML only.</li><li>Inspect the recovered file list and any skipped-image notices. A repeated picture uses one file with multiple numbered positions. Word alternative text is included for your review; missing text needs your decision.</li><li>Acknowledge that the ZIP contains the converted article and original image files, then choose <strong>Download images &amp; placement notes</strong> before importing. Applying or clearing the import discards the prepared package from the tab.</li><li>Extract the ZIP on your device. After taking the article into a WordPress draft, upload the pictures to its Media Library. Follow README.txt and image-map.json to replace each [Word image N] placeholder with an Image block. Check alternative text and preview before publishing.</li></ol>
<p>Recovery covers the first 200 visible image or object positions and up to 40 supported files, each at most 4 MiB and together at most 12 MiB. Unsupported, missing, damaged, or oversized images stay as placeholders. Images are neither displayed nor uploaded here, and linked images are not fetched. Original files can contain metadata and areas hidden by Word cropping. Crops, rotation, effects, captions, and page layout are not reproduced. The package describes this selected import, before later edits or combining drafts; keep the original document to recheck positions. Image files are excluded from private workspace backups and ordinary article exports.</p>
<p>You can also replace placeholders here before taking the blocks to WordPress. In More editing tools and draft history, choose Add or edit image, then Match a downloaded Word image package. Open the extracted image-map.json, compare each original number and article occurrence, and explicitly reuse reviewed Word alternative text. Paste each uploaded HTTPS URL, preview, and apply. Choose linked images separately before export. <a href="/help/publishing-handoff/#image-editor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Image matching steps and limits (new tab)</a>.</p>
<h2 id="word-import-questions">Common questions</h2>
<p>Try a complete fictional .docx, diagnose a rejected file, or check a saved post with the <a href="/help/conversion-checks/#word" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Word troubleshooting walkthrough (new tab)</a>. Save current writing before navigating away.</p>
<details><summary>Is this Word to WordPress converter free?</summary><p>Yes. The public workflow needs no account, payment, WordPress connection, API credits, or private AI pilot access. Opening a file, converting, reviewing, and preparing exports run in this tab.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does my document leave this page?</summary><p>No document upload or server conversion happens here. Selected bytes and pending previews stay temporarily in the tab and are cleared on apply or clearing the import. Article text remains after apply. Browser recovery is off by default and requires explicit consent to keep an unencrypted local copy. Keep a downloaded private backup for important work. No account history is provided. Links you choose to open are handled by their destination.</p></details>
<details><summary>Will the Word layout look identical in WordPress?</summary><p>No. This tool prepares editable article structure. It omits or simplifies document-only content, styling, and page layout. Inspect the conversion and its notices, keep the original .docx, and check the actual WordPress post before publishing. It does not verify facts, grammar, source quality, or image permissions.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I use a Google Docs draft?</summary><p>You can select an existing .docx exported by your document editor, or open Paste writing, keep source notes, or use more tools and choose Paste formatted writing. Inspect the conversion before applying it. This site does not connect to Google Drive.</p></details>
<details><summary>What if I already have Markdown?</summary><p>Use the same editor here or the <a href="/markdown-to-wordpress/">Markdown to WordPress converter</a>. Pasting into the article body keeps plain text or Markdown. The separate formatted import handles rich selections. Save existing work before navigating to another tool.</p></details>
